# Multiple Regression

## Multiple Regression

So far, we've only examined the relationship between a continuous outcome and continuous predictor one at a time with *simple regression*. With *multiple regression*, we can examine these relationships **jointly**: looking at one relationship while adjusting for the relationships of other variables.

$$y_i = \beta_0 \, + \, \beta_1 x_{1i} \, + \, \beta_2 x_{2i} \, + \, ... \, + \, \beta_p x_{2i} \, + \, \epsilon$$

::: subpoint
○ $\beta_0$: value of $y$ when *all predictors* $x = 0$
:::

::: subpoint
○ $\beta_1$: slope of $x_{1i}$ adjusted for the effect of $x_{2i}, \, ..., \,x_{pi}$ 
:::


## Simple Regression

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(palmerpenguins)

m1 <- lm(body_mass_g ~ bill_length_mm, data = penguins) 
summary(m1)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

Call:
lm(formula = body_mass_g ~ bill_length_mm, data = penguins)

Residuals:
     Min       1Q   Median       3Q      Max 
-1762.08  -446.98    32.59   462.31  1636.86 

Coefficients:
               Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)    
(Intercept)     362.307    283.345   1.279    0.202    
bill_length_mm   87.415      6.402  13.654   <2e-16 ***
---
Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1

Residual standard error: 645.4 on 340 degrees of freedom
  (2 observations deleted due to missingness)
Multiple R-squared:  0.3542,	Adjusted R-squared:  0.3523 
F-statistic: 186.4 on 1 and 340 DF,  p-value: < 2.2e-16
```


:::
:::

:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
The estimated penguin body mass when bill length is zero is 362.31.

An increase in 1 mm in bill length is associated with an 87.42 g increase in body mass.
:::
::::


## Standardizing


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
penguins_cleaned <- penguins |>
  filter_out(when_any(is.na(bill_length_mm), is.na(bill_depth_mm), 
                      is.na(flipper_length_mm), is.na(body_mass_g))) |>
    mutate(across(ends_with("_mm"), function(col) (col - mean(col)) / sd(col)))
penguins_cleaned
```

