:::
::::


## Residuals

We want to see that our residuals are normally distributed and have **constant variance across all levels of the predictors**.

Can extract residuals from a model with `resid(lm_model)` or `residuals(lm_model)`.

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="33%"}
... `pivot_longer()` the columns with the predictor values (`names(coef(lm_model))`) so each predictor has a row: then you can just `facet_wrap()` by the predictor name!
:::

::: {.column width="66%"}

::: {.panel-tabset}
### Plot

::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](lab_13_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-11-1.svg){fig-align='center' width=624}
:::
:::


### Code

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
penguins_cleaned |>
  add_column(residuals = residuals(m3)) |>
  pivot_longer(any_of(names(coef(m3))),
               names_to = "predictor", values_to = "x") |>
  ggplot(aes(x = x, y = residuals)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap("predictor") +
  theme_classic()
```
:::

:::


:::
::::



# Multicollinearity

## Multicollinearity

Multicollinearity refers to the ability of *predictors* to be associated with *each other*.

High multicollinearity: when the predictors in the model can explain a high amount of variance in another predictor. 

![](../media/lab_13/multicollinearity_gossip.png)

